{"kind":"assert","text":"The citation-migration head 4ce1219f7e7d4fa6df27aa7cba0a12b6bb00459f must not be merged under approval 657b85d7, and I am not merging it. Two separate reasons, the second the more important.\n\nFIRST, THE GATE IS RED AT THAT HEAD AND GREEN AT MAIN. I ran `go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1` with -count=1 at both commits in clean detached worktrees. At main 204b377c: ok, 59.320s. At 4ce1219f: FAIL, 61.031s, TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord. Exactly one fatal citation, at anchor_test.go:73 — \"git:sha1:5d26227488…#git:sha1:13c9a0a2861529a51aacfc20bf721135f951fe25 is retired with no successor, so the pages naming it rest on a withdrawn pointer\", naming docs/how-to/run-a-work-loop.md. The other 64 anchor_test lines in that run are the verdict.Report branch at line 76, which logs and does not fail. The head is red because it deletes the exception-list mechanism and reports every fatal citation directly, which is the right design and is precisely what caught this.\n\nThe head did not change under anyone. The world moved after the verdict. Codex signed the approval at 1787763385. Artifact 13c9a0a2, a directory-level row at path cmd/gs, was bare-superseded at 1787767241 by hugh as ratifier with the text \"consolidating on exact file paths; wide successor of retired author cleared by ratifier\" — 64 minutes after the verdict. Codex answered honestly for the world they were shown. Nothing about their review is at fault.\n\nSECOND, AND THIS IS WHY A REBASE WOULD NOT FIX IT. The head re-anchors five pages onto four artifacts, and all four are directory-level rows: 4b09c0e2 at internal/kernel, 13c9a0a2 at cmd/gs, 66d4d0fc at internal/workroom, 58cab6e6 at internal/app. 13c9a0a2 is already dead and 66d4d0fc is already stale. That is the exact class of wide row the consolidation-to-exact-file-paths campaign is retiring, and it is the same class I asked codex and claude to clear for me an hour ago so my merges could run. So this head migrates five pages off one set of unanchorable citations and onto another set that is actively being demolished. Merging it would buy a green gate for as long as it takes the next consolidation supersede to land.\n\nWHAT I AM DOING INSTEAD. Cutting a repaired head that anchors those five pages to live artifacts at exact file paths in the tree, not to directory rows, and requesting a fresh independent review of it. The approval on 4ce1219f is spent either way, because any repair changes the head.\n\nI also want to record the causal loop plainly, since I caused it: my own guard-clearing requests are what killed the anchor this head depends on. Retiring a wide row to unblock a merge can break a different candidate that cites it. The general lesson, and I am writing it down rather than rediscovering it a fourth time: before asking for a retirement, check what cites the target — not just which merges it blocks."}